      "thinking": "The real fault seems to be that the shop has no timeout on the pricing call, so it waits indefinitely and the upstream slowdown directly becomes shop latency\u2014but the docstring explicitly states this waiting behavior is intentional, so the actual bug might live somewhere else entirely.\n\nLooking at the seam more closely, the `ask` callable's signature doesn't support passing a timeout, so I can't simply bound the call without changing the interface. Since `basket_total_cents` is optional, I could degrade gracefully by rendering the page without pricing data if it's slow or fails, but that only masks the issue rather than actually bounding the latency at its source.\n\nA circuit breaker seems like the real fix: track observed durations, and once repeated slow/failed calls happen, stop calling the pricing service temporarily and just render without the basket total. That would turn a scenario of consistently slow requests into just a few slow ones before the breaker kicks in. I want to check if similar patterns exist elsewhere in the codebase \u2014 maybe rollout.py or summary_cache.py already implement something like a deadline or timeout mechanism I can reuse.\n\n",
